      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-242788",
      ":question/problem": "![](images/ab/ab254755c92db80349690f2db53e6258191e300fb385de64a85b1bf552f63728.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, $A_{1}$ and $C_{1}$ are the midpoints of $BC^(\u2015)$ and $AB^(\u2015)$, respectively. You may assume without proof that\n\n$\\frac{BC}{BA_{1}} = 2, \\frac{BA}{BC_{1}} = 2$.\n\nNow, consider the following statement:\n\n> $A_{1}C_{1}^(\u2015)\u2225AC^(\u2015)$\n\nThe table below gives the proof of the statement. Select the correct options in the table and thus complete the proof.\n\n|  | Statement | Reason |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | $\\frac{BC}{BA_{1}} = 2$ | Given |\n| 2 | $\\frac{BA}{BC_{1}} = 2$ | Given |\n| 3 | {{field-1}} | Transitive property of equality |\n| 4 | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |\n| 5 | $\u25b3ABC\u223c\u25b3C_{1}BA_{1}$ | SAS similarity criterion |\n| 6 | $\u2220BC_{1}A_{1}\u2245\u2220A$ | {{field-4}} |\n| 7 | $A_{1}C_{1}^(\u2015)\u2225AC^(\u2015)$ | Converse corresponding angles postulate |"
    },
