      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-242789",
      ":question/problem": "![](images/98/9899839c9ec5abfb8654f31b2ef8346e68c6ec5eb0a19608bc3a6ab1c16121b7.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, $M$ and $N$ are the midpoints of $BC^(\u2015)$ and $BA^(\u2015)$, respectively. You may assume without proof that\n\n$\\frac{BN}{BA} = \\frac{1}{2}$.\n\nNow, consider the following statement:\n\n> $MN^(\u2015)\u2225AC^(\u2015)$\n\nThe table below gives the proof of the statement. Select the correct options in the table and thus complete the proof.\n\n|  | Statement | Reason |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | $\\frac{BN}{BA} = \\frac{1}{2}$ | Given |\n| 2 | $M$ is the midpoint of $BC^(\u2015)$ | Given |\n| 3 | $BM = \\frac{1}{2}BC$ | Definition of a midpoint |\n| 4 | $\\frac{BM}{BC} = \\frac{1}{2}$ | Multiplication principle |\n| 5 | $\\frac{BN}{BA} = \\frac{BM}{BC}$ | Transitive property of equality |\n| 6 | $\u2220ABC\u2245\u2220NBM$ | Reflexive property of congruence |\n| 7 | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |\n| 8 | {{field-3}} | Corresponding angles in similar triangles |\n| 9 | $MN^(\u2015)\u2225AC^(\u2015)$ | {{field-4}} |"
